WORLD BANK IMPLEMENTATION COMPLETION AND RESULTS REPORT
Bihar Rural Roads Project (P155522)
Report No: ICR00006014 | Credit Number: 5938-IN
Country: India | Sector: Transport | Amount: US$235 million
Overall Outcome Rating: Moderately Unsatisfactory
Date: June 22, 2024 | Task Team Leader: N.S. Srinivas
Source: documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099062724045515028

PROJECT DEVELOPMENT OBJECTIVE:
To improve rural road connectivity in project districts, and enhance management of rural roads in Bihar.

CONTEXT:
Bihar was one of the faster-growing low-income states in India, with 34 percent of its population living below
the poverty line. About 90 percent of its population of 104 million was rural and about 76 percent in north Bihar
was subject to recurring flood threats. About 60 percent of Bihar's rural road network was unpaved, with missing
links, dilapidated bridges, inadequate geometry, poor drainage, weak pavements, and missing road safety measures.

KEY IMPLEMENTATION FACTORS AND LESSONS LEARNED:

1. PROCUREMENT AND CONTRACTING DELAYS WERE THE PRIMARY IMPLEMENTATION BOTTLENECK
The project experienced significant procurement delays averaging 14-18 months beyond planned timelines.
Single-source bidding in remote districts and weak contractor capacity in Bihar's market were the primary causes.
The project underwent five restructurings, including four extensions of the closing date. Future projects in
similar contexts should pre-qualify contractors 18-24 months before project effectiveness and establish
regional contractor development programs to build local market capacity.

2. FLOOD RISK WAS SYSTEMATICALLY UNDERWEIGHTED IN DESIGN
76 percent of Bihar's population in north Bihar is exposed to annual flooding, yet climate resilience design
standards were not applied consistently across all road segments. Several road segments built to standard
specifications suffered damage in the first major flood season. The M&E system did not track flood damage
separately from general maintenance needs, making it impossible to isolate climate-related losses. Future
transport projects in flood-prone areas must apply climate screening at the sub-project level, not just at
the portfolio level.

3. ASSET MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS REQUIRE INSTITUTIONAL EMBEDDING BEFORE PROJECT CLOSE
The project developed an Asset Management System (AMS) and Online Management, Monitoring and Accounting
System (OMMAS) to improve road sector management. However, adoption by the Bihar Rural Road Development
Agency (BRRDA) remained partial at project close. The core lesson: digital systems introduced as project
components without dedicated change management resources are rarely sustained. The project should have
embedded AMS training and government ownership from Year 1 rather than treating it as a Year 3-4 activity.

4. OUTPUT-PERFORMANCE BASED MAINTENANCE CONTRACTS SHOWED PROMISE BUT NEED LONGER HORIZONS
The Output and Performance-Based Maintenance Contracts (OPBMCs) piloted under this project demonstrated
improved road condition outcomes compared to traditional maintenance contracts. However, 5-year contract
horizons were insufficient to demonstrate sustainability. Road maintenance contracts in South Asian contexts
should be designed for minimum 7-10 year periods to generate meaningful performance data and contractor
investment in quality.

5. GENDER DISAGGREGATED DATA WAS COLLECTED INCONSISTENTLY
The project included gender indicators but data collection was inconsistent across project districts.
Beneficiary surveys conducted in Year 4 showed that women's travel time savings from improved roads were
significantly higher than men's (averaging 28 minutes vs 14 minutes per trip) due to women's higher rates
of walking for market access and healthcare. This finding was not captured in Year 1-2 reporting.
Gender-disaggregated beneficiary tracking must be built into baseline surveys, not added retroactively.

6. FIDUCIARY AND ENVIRONMENTAL COMPLIANCE REQUIRED INTENSIVE SUPERVISION
Environmental compliance with Environmental Codes of Practice (ECoPs) was inconsistent in early years.
The Bank's Hands-on Expanded Implementation Support (HEIS) team visits to Bihar significantly improved
compliance in Years 3-5. Remote supervision alone was insufficient for environmental and social compliance
in large infrastructure programs. Future projects in similar contexts should budget for quarterly site visits
by environmental and social specialists throughout the project life.

KEY RESULTS ACHIEVED:
- Rural road connectivity improved for approximately 2.4 million beneficiaries
- 1,847 km of rural roads improved (vs target of 3,000 km due to cancellation)
- Road sector institutional capacity strengthened through AMS and training programs
- Economic Internal Rate of Return (EIRR): 18.3% (above 12% threshold)

RISK TO DEVELOPMENT OUTCOME: Substantial
Primary risks: (1) Inadequate maintenance funding from Government of Bihar post-project;
(2) Continued flood damage without climate-resilient design standards; (3) Partial adoption of AMS
limiting long-term asset management improvements.
